# XBD 5 File Format Notation
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# The chapter is the notation the utility pages use to describe the text
# they write (the STDOUT, STDERR and OUTPUT FILES sections) and, for printf
# and awk, the format strings those utilities accept. What a system can show
# is therefore the behaviour of those two utilities (their formats follow the
# chapter, with the exceptions their own pages list) and the exactness of the
# formats the utility pages state. The latter is tested in the utility
# modules and referenced from the .cov file.
#
# Conversions tested with printf and with awk (both must follow the chapter):
# the flags, the field width, the precision and the d i o u x X c s
# conversions. Floating-point conversions are tested with awk only, because
# the printf utility page lets an implementation leave them out. Every test
# runs in the POSIX locale. Results the chapter leaves to the implementation
# (the style of infinity and NaN, # on d i u c s, 0 on c and s, too few
# arguments) are never examined.

assertion  XBD.5-file-format-notation.001
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap05.html#5. File Format Notation
class      required
since      1992
says       A field width is a minimum: shorter output is padded on the left
           with spaces (on the right with the - flag), longer output is never
           cut. This holds for d, c and s.
test       t/001-field-width.sh

assertion  XBD.5-file-format-notation.002
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap05.html#5. File Format Notation
class      required
since      1992
says       The + flag puts a sign on a signed conversion; the space flag puts
           a space before a result that has no sign; given together, + wins.
test       t/002-sign-flags.sh

assertion  XBD.5-file-format-notation.003
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap05.html#5. File Format Notation
class      required
since      1992
says       When the 0 flag and the - flag are both given, the 0 flag has no
           effect: the number is padded with spaces on the right.
test       t/003-left-beats-zero.sh

assertion  XBD.5-file-format-notation.004
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap05.html#5. File Format Notation
class      required
since      1992
says       The precision of d, i, o, u, x and X is the least number of
           digits, filled with leading zeros, and never cuts a longer
           number; a sign comes before the zeros.
test       t/004-integer-precision.sh

assertion  XBD.5-file-format-notation.005
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap05.html#5. File Format Notation
class      required
since      1992
says       Converting zero with a precision of 0 (also a bare period, whose
           empty digit string means zero) writes no characters, but with the
           # flag on o it writes 0; without a precision zero writes the one
           digit 0.
test       t/005-zero-precision.sh

assertion  XBD.5-file-format-notation.006
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap05.html#5. File Format Notation
class      required
since      1992
says       For d, i, o, u, x and X, a precision makes the 0 flag have no
           effect: the width is filled with spaces.
test       t/006-precision-beats-zero.sh

assertion  XBD.5-file-format-notation.007
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap05.html#5. File Format Notation
class      required
since      1992
says       The 0 flag pads to the width with zeros that come after the sign
           (or space) and after the 0x or 0X prefix.
test       t/007-zero-padding.sh

assertion  XBD.5-file-format-notation.008
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap05.html#5. File Format Notation
class      required
since      1992
says       The # flag on o forces the first digit of the result to be a zero
           (8 gives 010, 0 stays 0), and on x and X prefixes a non-zero
           result with 0x or 0X (zero is written as 0).
test       t/008-alternative-form.sh

assertion  XBD.5-file-format-notation.009
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap05.html#5. File Format Notation
class      required
since      1992
says       The precision of s is the most bytes written, a bare period means
           zero bytes, and the width still applies to what is written.
test       t/009-string-precision.sh

assertion  XBD.5-file-format-notation.010
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap05.html#5. File Format Notation
class      required
since      1992
says       The f and F conversions write decimal notation with the number of
           digits after the radix character equal to the precision (six when
           it is omitted, no radix character at precision 0 unless # is
           given), with the sign, space, width, - and 0 flags as for
           integers.
note       Tested with the awk printf statement only: the printf utility page
           lets an implementation omit the floating-point conversions.
test       t/010-fixed-point.sh

assertion  XBD.5-file-format-notation.011
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap05.html#5. File Format Notation
class      required
since      1992
says       The e and E conversions write one digit, the radix character, the
           precision's worth of digits and an exponent of at least two digits
           introduced by e or E and a sign (more digits when the exponent
           needs them; six digits after the radix character by default).
note       Tested with the awk printf statement only, as for the f
           conversion.
test       t/011-exponent-form.sh

assertion  XBD.5-file-format-notation.012
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap05.html#5. File Format Notation
class      required
since      1992
says       The g and G conversions use the precision as the number of
           significant digits (six by default), the e style only when the
           exponent is below -4 or at least the precision, remove trailing
           zeros and a lone radix character, and keep them with the # flag.
note       Tested with the awk printf statement only, as for the f
           conversion.
test       t/012-general-form.sh

assertion  XBD.5-file-format-notation.013
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap05.html#5. File Format Notation
class      required
since      2001
says       The a and A conversions write the value zero with the digit 0
           and a zero exponent (0x0p+0 and 0X0P+0; any fraction digits are
           zeros) and
           another value, 255 here, as 0x (0X), one non-zero hexadecimal
           digit, optionally a radix character and more hexadecimal digits, p
           (P), a sign and a decimal exponent without leading zeros, with
           lowercase letters for a and uppercase letters for A.
note       Tested with the awk printf statement only, as for the f
           conversion. The number of hexadecimal digits and the choice of the
           first one for a non-zero value are not examined.
test       t/013-hex-float.sh

assertion  XBD.5-file-format-notation.014
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap05.html#5. File Format Notation
class      required
since      1992
says       Arguments left over once the format is used up are ignored by the
           awk printf statement (the printf utility instead reuses the
           format; see XCU.printf.004).
test       t/014-excess-arguments.sh
